          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Sheep primarily symbolize God’s people—Israel, believers, or the church—as the flock dependent on God or Christ the Shepherd for guidance, protection, and care.\",\"book_summary\":\"Sheep are the covenant people, God’s flock, who hear the Shepherd’s voice and follow him.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"God’s people as the flock of God or Christ.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The covenant people as the Shepherd’s flock.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement. The book emphasizes hearing and following the Shepherd, while the consensus also discusses wayward or lost sheep in particular contexts; this does not alter the shared primary identification.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. Both identify sheep centrally with God’s covenant people under the divine Shepherd.\",\"rationale\":\"The book’s “covenant people—His flock” is equivalent to the consensus identification of Israel, the church, or believers as God’s people and flock. Hearing the Shepherd’s voice and following him is a standard feature of that same symbolism, explicitly recognized by the consensus through John 10. The book is more concise but does not materially narrow, broaden, or replace the referent.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The wording could be read as limiting sheep to responsive covenant members who actually hear and follow, potentially excluding lost or wayward sheep. However, in context it most naturally states a defining pastoral relationship rather than a strict exclusion, and the cited texts directly support the shared core.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
